WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.767
Decorators.

2
00:00:02.067 --> 00:00:06.714
Every annotation you will meet in Python frameworks is secretly the same trick.

3
00:00:06.714 --> 00:00:08.067
Let's see how it works.

4
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:13.894
A decorator is a function that takes a function, and returns a replacement.

5
00:00:13.894 --> 00:00:16.300
Usually, that replacement is a wrapper.

6
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.393
The at timed line above a def is just sugar.

7
00:00:19.393 --> 00:00:21.804
It means: fetch equals timed of fetch.

8
00:00:21.804 --> 00:00:24.533
The name now points at the wrapped version.

9
00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:28.000
The wrapper remembers the original function through a closure.

10
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.167
Here's what happens when you call a decorated function.

11
00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:36.733
The caller uses the same name as always, but now it reaches the wrapper.

12
00:00:37.033 --> 00:00:38.514
First, code runs before.

13
00:00:38.514 --> 00:00:39.933
Here, we start a timer.

14
00:00:40.233 --> 00:00:43.233
Then the wrapper calls the original function, untouched.

15
00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:45.175
Afterwards, code runs again.

16
00:00:45.175 --> 00:00:46.700
We print how long it took.

17
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.567
And the original result goes back to the caller.

18
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:52.767
Here is a timing decorator in full.

19
00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:57.967
The inner wrapper takes any arguments, calls the original, and measures the time around it.

20
00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:02.267
The wraps line copies the original name and docstring onto the wrapper.

21
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:06.533
Then one line, at timed, applies it to our NEO feed function.

22
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:10.300
Calling it prints the time, about zero point four seconds.

23
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.400
Here's where decorators earn their keep.

24
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:18.100
An agent has four API tools, and each one needs retry logic.

25
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.133
Instead of pasting it four times, you write retry once and stack it on each function.

26
00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:25.768
Your FastAPI routes work the same way.

27
00:01:25.768 --> 00:01:29.700
The app dot get line registers your function with the framework.

28
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.362
The classic mistake is forgetting wraps.

29
00:01:33.362 --> 00:01:38.900
Then every decorated function is anonymously called wrapper, and your logs become useless.

30
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.507
The fix is one line.

31
00:01:40.507 --> 00:01:44.167
Use functools wraps, and the original identity survives.

32
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:46.139
Let's recap.

33
00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:49.033
A decorator wraps a function and replaces it.

34
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:52.067
The at sign is just sugar for reassigning the name.

35
00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:54.067
Always use functools wraps.

36
00:01:54.367 --> 00:01:57.344
And write a policy once, apply it everywhere.

37
00:01:57.344 --> 00:01:58.667
Next up: generators.
